706	Recurring EBM Tablet Failure (Staff: Odile, Gatsibo Site)	Incident	<p><strong>Dear ED,</strong></p><p>Below is the incident report regarding the issue of the tablet used for EBM activities by Gatsibo field staff member, Odile.</p><h3><strong>1. Overview</strong></h3><p>Odile, a field staff member at the Gatsibo site, has raised the same issue many times over an extended period: her tablet, used for EBM invoicing, shuts down unexpectedly and frequently, especially when trying to generate an invoice. This has caused significant delays in invoicing and financial losses.</p><h3><strong>2. Previous Interventions &amp; Failures</strong></h3><p>Our team and the Rwanda Revenue Authority (RRA) have attempted multiple fixes:</p><ul><li>Reboot (advised by RRA): <strong>Failed</strong></li><li>Formatting (advised by RRA): <strong>Failed</strong></li><li>Diagnostic &amp; maintenance: <strong>Failed again</strong></li><li>RRA recommendation to replace tablet: <strong>Delayed; tablet continued failing</strong></li></ul><p>After any temporary fix, the tablet works for&nbsp;less than two minutes&nbsp;before shutting down again. The problem remains completely unsolved.</p><h3><strong>3. Proposed Next Step: Using a Laptop</strong></h3><p>Installing EBM on a laptop could be considered, but this option can be rejected due to:</p><ul><li><strong>Conflict with daily work:</strong>&nbsp;The laptop is already needed for QuickBooks, RWVCA Portal reporting, and other activities. Running EBM on it would likely cause application shutdowns.</li><li><strong>Penalty risk:</strong>&nbsp;If the EBM application is deleted or goes missing from the laptop, the RRA imposes a&nbsp;200,000 RWF fine.</li></ul><h3><strong>4. Consequences</strong></h3><ul><li>Delayed invoice generation</li><li>Financial losses for the site</li><li>Repeated frustration for Odile</li><li>Risk of RRA sanctions</li></ul><h3><strong>5. Recommended Solution</strong></h3><ul><li>Stop attempting repairs on the faulty tablet immediately.</li><li>Provide Odile with a&nbsp;new Windows OS tablet that supports a SIM card, paired with a&nbsp;Bluetooth POS printer, no laptop required, fully portable.</li></ul><h3><strong>6. Summary &amp; Request</strong></h3><p>In all, Odile's EBM tablet at the Gatsibo site has failed repeatedly despite multiple RRA-guided repairs (reboot, format, maintenance). It now works for less than two minutes per attempt. Using a laptop risks a 200,000 RWF penalty and conflicts with her daily reporting tasks. The only viable solution is to replace the faulty tablet with a new Windows OS tablet (SIM-capable) and a Bluetooth POS printer.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Kind Regards,</strong></p>	2026-06-01	2026-06-02	2	2026-06-02 16:39:13+00	2026-06-02 16:39:13+00	17:00:00	18:00:00	RWVCA Head Office
